faqs:
  - question: "What is the Appwrite and Entire partnership?"
    answer: "It's a collaboration between Appwrite and Entire, two companies that build in the open, to connect Entire's Git platform for agent-driven development with Appwrite's hosting and backend. As part of it, Entire becomes a Git provider for Appwrite Sites and Functions on Appwrite Cloud: connect an Entire project, pick a repository, and every push builds and deploys."
  - question: "What is Entire?"
    answer: "Entire is a Git forge built for development with coding agents. It hosts repositories on its own Git network across several regions and stores the agent sessions behind each change next to the code, so a commit can carry the conversation that produced it. Its CLI is open source under the MIT license. Learn more at [entire.io](https://entire.io)."
  - question: "How do I connect an Entire repository to Appwrite?"
    answer: "In the Appwrite Console, go to **Project settings** > **Git configuration** and click **Connect to Entire**, or click **Connect Entire** in the Site or Function creation wizard. Approve the installation on entire.io, choose the repositories Appwrite can see, then create a Site or Function from one of them. The [step-by-step guide](/blog/post/deploy-from-entire-to-appwrite) covers the full flow."
  - question: "Does Appwrite create preview deployments for Entire branches?"
    answer: "Yes. Pushes to your production branch build and activate. Pushes to any other branch build without activating, and Sites get a [preview link](/docs/products/sites/previews) that only members of your Appwrite organization can open."
  - question: "How do I see Appwrite build results on Entire?"
    answer: "Appwrite writes a check run to every commit it builds. The check moves from starting to building to succeeded or failed, and its summary links to the deployment in the Appwrite Console and, for Sites, to the running preview."
  - question: "Can I create a new Entire repository from the Appwrite Console?"
    answer: "No. Appwrite's connection to Entire reads repository contents and writes check runs, but it cannot create repositories. Create the repository on entire.io or with `entire repo create` in the Entire CLI first, then connect it from the Site or Function wizard."
  - question: "Is Entire available on self-hosted Appwrite?"
    answer: "Entire is available as a Git provider on [Appwrite Cloud](/sign-in). The self-hosted Appwrite Console does not offer new Entire connections yet."
---

Before coding agents, every feature was written by hand. Turning an idea into working code took hours, often days, and everything around it moved at the same pace: contributions arrived a few at a time, and pull requests waited their turn in review.

Coding agents have changed that pace. Developers now delegate whole features to an agent and review what comes back, and a single session can produce a steady stream of commits and pull requests. Keeping up takes better tools: ones that explain why each change was made, and show whether it works as soon as it's pushed.

Today, we're announcing a partnership between Appwrite and Entire to bring those tools together. As part of it, **Entire joins Appwrite's deployment providers**, so a push to an Entire repository can build and deploy Appwrite Sites and Functions.

# Why Appwrite and Entire are partnering

Every change an agent pushes raises two questions: how did this get written, and does it work? Entire answers the first, and Appwrite answers the second.

Entire is the Git forge built for agents. It hosts repositories on its own network, in regions close to your team, and keeps the agent session behind each change next to the code. Every commit an agent makes becomes a checkpoint that pairs the change with the conversation that produced it. Work is organized into **trails**, Entire's unit of work, which follow one branch from the first intent to shipped code.

Teams don't have to move their code to start using it, either. Entire can mirror an existing GitHub repository without moving the source of truth: GitHub stays the upstream, agents clone from the nearest Entire region, and pushes through the mirror are forwarded back to GitHub.

Appwrite is an open-source developer platform with authentication, databases, storage, serverless functions, realtime, messaging, and web hosting in one place. [Sites](/docs/products/sites) and [Functions](/docs/products/functions) deploy straight from Git: a push becomes a build, and a build becomes a live site or API.

Together, Entire and Appwrite cover the whole path from an agent's commit to a running app. Entire keeps the code and the reasoning behind every change. Appwrite builds that code into sites and APIs, backed by authentication, databases, and storage in the same project, and reports each result back to the commit that triggered it. For teams building with agents, that means one workflow from the first prompt to production, where every change can be traced, verified, and shipped.

# Entire is now a deployment provider for Sites and Functions

You can connect an Entire project to your Appwrite project, pick a repository, and every push builds and deploys. Here's what that includes on **Appwrite Cloud**:

- **Connect each Entire project separately.** Connect Entire from the Appwrite Console and approve the installation on entire.io. You can give Appwrite access to all of the project's repositories, including ones you add later, or only the ones you select. You can change that selection whenever you need to.
- **Push to deploy.** Commits to your production branch build and activate. Commits to any other branch build without activating, and Sites get a [preview link](/docs/products/sites/previews) that only members of your Appwrite organization can open.
- **Build results on Entire.** Appwrite reports every build back to Entire as a check run on the commit. The check moves from starting to building to succeeded or failed, and its summary links to the deployment in the Console and, for Sites, to the running preview.
- **The same build controls.** [Branch and path filters](/blog/post/announcing-git-deployment-triggers), root directories, install and build commands, and environment variables work exactly as they do for any Git-connected Site or Function.
- **Links that resolve to Entire.** Deployments link to the repository, branch, and commit on entire.io, using Entire's own URL paths.
- **Narrow access.** Appwrite asks Entire for read access to repository contents and write access to checks, and only for the repositories you select. If you uninstall Appwrite from your Entire project, Appwrite disconnects the Sites and Functions that deployed from it.

**Ready to get started?** Our [guide to deploying from Entire](/blog/post/deploy-from-entire-to-appwrite) walks through creating an Entire repository, connecting it to Appwrite, and shipping a site and a function from it.

# How both communities benefit

For the **Appwrite community**, this partnership brings a Git home built for agent work, without a migration. Entire connects alongside the Git providers your project already uses, so you can move a single Site or Function to an Entire repository and keep its domains, environment variables, and build settings as they are. What you gain is context: each deployment links to its commit on Entire, where the agent session that produced the change sits next to the code. When a build fails or a release misbehaves, you can see why the change was made, not just what changed.

For the **Entire community**, this partnership opens a complete backend, not just hosting. Code on Entire can become a production site or an API endpoint without a separate deploy script, and when the app needs more, [Auth](/docs/products/auth), [Databases](/docs/products/databases), [Storage](/docs/products/storage), and [Realtime](/docs/apis/realtime) are already in the same Appwrite project.

# Deploying from Entire on Appwrite Cloud

Entire is available as a deployment provider on Appwrite Cloud today. Connect your Entire project from the Console, pick a repository, and push.

- [Deploy from Entire to Appwrite](/blog/post/deploy-from-entire-to-appwrite) walks through the full setup, from creating the repository to branch previews and path filters.
- [Deploy Sites from Git](/docs/products/sites/deploy-from-git) and [Deploy Functions from Git](/docs/products/functions/deploy-from-git) cover build triggers, build commands, and root directories.
- [Sign up for Entire](https://entire.com/login) to create a project and your first repository.
- [Sign up for Appwrite Cloud](/sign-up) to connect your first Entire project.
